<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Manuel I of Portugal</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1469">1469</a>–<a href="/years/1521">1521</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Manuel the Fortunate · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-aviz">House of Aviz</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Manuel I was King of Portugal from 1495 to 1521, known as the Fortunate. His reign saw <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vasco-da-gama">Vasco da Gama</a>'s voyage to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, Cabral's landing in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a> and the building of a Portuguese empire in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-ocean">Indian Ocean</a>, as well as fortified posts on the Atlantic coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a>. Spice-trade wealth paid for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manueline-style">Manueline style</a> of architecture named after him.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/manuel-i-of-portugal.jpg" alt="Manuel I of Portugal" width="320" height="473" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Gilles Messian from Paris, France · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Statue_of_King_Manuel_I_of_Portugal_by_Nicolau_Chanterene.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Manuel was born at Alcochete on 31 May 1469, the ninth child of Ferdinand, Duke of Viseu, and Beatriz of Portugal. He grew up amid strife between the noble families and King John II: in 1483 Fernando II, Duke of Braganza, was executed for treason, and in 1484 Manuel's older brother Diogo, accused of a conspiracy, was stabbed to death by the king himself. After the death of his son Prince Afonso, John II named his cousin Manuel heir, and Manuel succeeded him in 1495.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Manuel supported Atlantic exploration and commerce. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vasco-da-gama">Vasco da Gama</a> reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> by sea in 1498, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pedro-alvares-cabral">Pedro Álvares Cabral</a> voyaged to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a> in 1500, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francisco-de-almeida">Francisco de Almeida</a> became the first viceroy of India in 1505. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afonso-de-albuquerque">Afonso de Albuquerque</a> took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goa">Goa</a> in 1510, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malacca">Malacca</a> in 1511 and Ormus in 1515, and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a> the Portuguese took Safi in 1508 and Azamor in 1513. Manuel founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/casa-da-india">Casa da Índia</a> to manage the monopolies, and the trade made him the wealthiest monarch in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. Royal absolutism was the method of government, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corte">Cortes</a> met only four times.</p><p>His religious policy was harsh. In 1496, while seeking to marry Isabella of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-aragon">Aragon</a>, he yielded to pressure from her parents and decreed that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> who refused baptism must leave; then, before the deadline, he converted all Jews by royal decree. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lisbon">Lisbon</a> massacre of 1506 thousands of accused Jews were murdered, and Manuel executed the leaders of the riot. He also ordered the expulsion of Muslims from Portugal.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Manuel married three times, each time to a member of the Spanish royal family: Isabella, who died in childbirth in 1498, then her sister Maria, and finally Eleanor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>. He died on 13 December 1521, aged 52, after a fever, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lisbon">Lisbon</a> suffered an outbreak of plague, and was succeeded by his son John III. His remains were moved to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jeronimos-monastery">Jerónimos Monastery</a> in 1551. He financed the Monastery of Jerónimos and patronised the playwright Gil Vicente, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manueline-style">Manueline style</a> of architecture is named for him.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Manuel I of Portugal” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1512 King <b>Manuel I of Portugal</b> had the garrison withdrawn, and Kilwa remained only a nominal vassal.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kilwa-sultanate">Kilwa Sultanate · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Under <b>Manuel I</b> the Portuguese built or seized Atlantic posts at Agadir, Safi, Azemmour and Mazagan between 1505 and 1514.</blockquote><a href="/state/portuguese-morocco">Portuguese Morocco</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lisbon">Lisbon</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/portugal">Portugal</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lisbon">Lisbon</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/portugal">Portugal</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=38.71,-9.14&amp;year=1502" aria-label="Lisbon on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Manuel I of Portugal"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4393 4234h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4393 4234h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=38.71,-9.14&amp;year=1502">What was here in 1502? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
